[Tiny Little Office](https://tinylittleoffice.com/), from Aboard, is a collection of small browser applications: a word processor, spreadsheet, presentation builder, drawing tool, and more. The fun bit is where the document lives. Its contents are encoded in the URL, so copying the address copies the document. No account needed.

This takes the [URL-as-state idea I wrote about earlier](/posts/20251226-url-state-management/) somewhere very literal. You're saving the document to the address bar. Bookmark it to keep a copy; send the link to give someone else one. Open it, make a change, and the resulting URL carries that version.

It also reminds me of [Pinment](/posts/20260225-introducing-pinment/), my bookmarklet for pinning comments to webpages and passing the review session around in a URL. I like these tiny, portable applications where handing someone the work is as straightforward as handing them a link.

Tiny Little Office looks especially appealing for the small things I want to send back and forth: a quick outline, a few slides, a little table to work something out. The recipient can edit their copy and return a new link. Each link carries a version, so keeping track of which one you're discussing still matters.

There's a practical size limit to putting a document in a URL, of course. But for small exchanges, this is a lovely use of the browser. I'd like to see more tools make that much of the address bar.

